<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch9-section-setup-subsection-general-vertical-coordinate" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>General vertical coordinate</title>

    <p>
        Consider our usual vertical coordinate: <m>z</m>, the geometric height.
        As shown in <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z" text="type-global" /> below, <m>z</m> strictly increases with increasing height.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z">
        <caption>
            Vertical cross-section through several <m>z</m>-surfaces at a single moment in time,
            with equal increment <m>\delta z</m> between each surface.
            The <m>+x</m> and <m>+z</m> axes are shown,
            and the <m>+y</m>-axis points into the page.
            Given that <m>\delta z \gt 0</m>,
            the values of the <m>z</m>-surfaces increase in the <m>+z</m>-direction.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch9-vertical-coordinate-z.png" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>
                Four horizontal lines stacked vertically representing geometric heights
                with an equal increment of height between them.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Say a state variable <m>s = s(x,y,z,t)</m> is strictly monotonic with respect to <m>z</m>
        (i.e., <m>s</m> either strictly increases or strictly decreases with increasing <m>z</m>).
        This means <m>z = z(x,y,s,t)</m> since strictly monotonic functions are invertible,
        and so a state variable <m>f = f(x,y,z,t)</m> becomes <m>f = f(x,y,z(x,y,s,t),t)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Furthermore,
        because <m>s</m> is strictly monotonic with respect to <m>z</m>,
        we can use <m>s</m> rather than <m>z</m> as the vertical coordinate!
    </p>

    <p>
        We can visualize <m>s</m> as a surface,
        such as an <term>isobaric surface</term> (a surface of constant pressure <m>p</m>) if <m>s</m> is replaced with <m>p</m>
        or an <term>isentropic surface</term> (a surface of constant potential temperature <m>\theta</m>) if <m>s</m> is replaced with <m>\theta</m>.
        Such surfaces are "wavy" and evolve with time.
        A vertical slice through several such surfaces at one moment in time is shown in <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s" text="type-global" /> below,
        with the <m>z</m>-surfaces of <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z" text="type-global" /> included for reference.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s">
        <caption>
            Vertical cross-section through several <m>s</m>-surfaces at a single moment in time,
            with equal increment <m>\delta s</m> between each surface.
            The <m>+x</m> and <m>+z</m> axes are shown,
            and the <m>+y</m>-axis points into the page.
            If <m>\delta s \gt 0</m>,
            the values of the <m>s</m>-surfaces increase in the <m>+z</m>-direction;
            if <m>\delta s \lt 0</m>, the values of the <m>s</m>-surfaces decrease in the <m>+z</m>-direction.
            The <m>z</m>-surfaces of <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z" text="type-global" /> are included in the background for reference.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s.png" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>
                Four horizontal lines stacked vertically representing geometric heights
                with an equal increment of height between them.
                Four thicker wavy lines representing constant surfaces of another variable are superimposed.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Consider a state variable <m>f = f(x,y,z,t)</m>.
        As noted above,
        since <m>z = z(x,y,s,t)</m>,
        we can write <m>f = f(x,y,z(x,y,s,t),t)</m>.
        To find, for example, <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>,
        we can treat <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>, and <m>t</m> as intermediate variables
        and <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>s</m>, and <m>t</m> as independent variables and apply the chain rule for multivariable functions.
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
                \right)_{y,s,t}
                \amp =
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
                \right)_{y,z,t}
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial x}{\partial x}
                \right)_{y,s,t}
                +
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}
                \right)_{x,y,t}
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
                \right)_{y,s,t}
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-chain-rule-x-s-full" number="yes">
                \amp =
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
                \right)_{y,z,t}
                +
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}
                \right)_{x,y,t}
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
                \right)_{y,s,t}
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Note the terms <m>\left( \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y} \right)_{x,z,t} \left( \dfrac{\partial y}{\partial x} \right)_{y,s,t}</m>
        and <m>\left( \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial t} \right)_{x,y,z} \left( \dfrac{\partial t}{\partial x} \right)_{y,s,t}</m>
        are omitted:
        This is because they are 0 since <m>y</m> and <m>t</m> do not vary with <m>x</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        For brevity,
        and to align with other texts,
        the subscripts "y" and "t" are omitted from <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-x-s-full" text="type-global" />
        since these variables are held constant for all the partial derivatives that appear in the equation.
        Then <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-x-s-full" text="type-global" /> can be written somewhat more succinctly as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-chain-rule-x-s-reduced" number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
            \right)_{s}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            +
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}
            \right)_{x}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{s}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Many texts justify <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-x-s-reduced" text="type-global" />
        physically by "zooming in" on a portion of <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s" text="type-global" />,
        as the dashed box indicates in <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-box" text="type-global" /> below.
        <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-zoom" text="type-global" />
        zooms in on the portion of <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-box" text="type-global" /> inside the dashed box.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-box">
        <caption>
            Reproduction of <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s" text="type-global" />,
            where the dashed blue box indicates the portion of the figure chosen for closer inspection
            in <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-zoom" text="type-global" />.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-box.png" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>
                Four horizontal lines stacked vertically representing geometric heights
                with an equal increment of height between them.
                Four thicker wavy lines representing constant surfaces of another variable are superimposed.
                A dashed box highlights a portion of one wavy line.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-zoom">
        <caption>
            Zoomed-in view of the region enclosed by the dashed blue box in <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-box" text="type-global" />,
            with state variable <m>f</m> measured at locations <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>.
            Locations <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are separated by horizontal distance <m>\delta x</m>,
            and locations <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> are separated by vertical distance <m>\delta z</m>.
            Locations <m>A</m> and <m>C</m> are found along the same surface <m>s</m>.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-zoom.png" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>
                Two parallel horizontal lines representing geometric heights,
                and a line cutting diagonally across them representing a constant surface of another variable.
                Intersections are marked by dots,
                and an additional dot creates a right triangle.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        In <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-zoom" text="type-global" />,
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \frac{f_C - f_A}{\delta x}
                \amp =
                \frac{f_C - f_B + f_B - f_A}{\delta x}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                \frac{f_C - f_B}{\delta x}
                +
                \frac{f_B - f_A}{\delta x}
            </mrow>
            <mrow number="yes">
                \amp =
                \frac{f_C - f_B}{\delta z} \frac{\delta z}{\delta x}
                +
                \frac{f_B - f_A}{\delta x}
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Note from <xref ref="ch9-vertical-coordinate-z-s-zoom" text="type-global" />
        that <m>\dfrac{f_C - f_B}{\delta z}</m> is calculated at constant <m>x</m>,
        <m>\dfrac{f_B - f_A}{\delta x}</m> is calculated at constant <m>z</m>,
        and both <m>\dfrac{f_C - f_A}{\delta x}</m> and <m>\dfrac{\delta z}{\delta x}</m>
        are calculated along a surface of constant <m>s</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        In the limit as <m>\delta x \to 0</m> (and so <m>\delta z \to 0</m>), we have
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
            \right)_{s}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}
            \right)_{x}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{s}
            +
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        which is equivalent to <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-x-s-reduced" text="type-global" />.
    </p>

    <p>
        Similar arguments apply for <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> and produce
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
            \right)_{s}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
            \right)_{z}
            +
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}
            \right)_{y}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}
            \right)_{s}
        </md>
    </p>

</subsection>